    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I've implemented `publish_run` in `app/publish.py`, but the large-upload and resume behaviour won't work in this `.venv` as it stands. That behaviour needs the `hf_xet` package, and it isn't installed here. Tests pass (2 passed with `pytest -q`), but they use a fake Hub client, so nothing was actually uploaded.\n\n**What it does**\n- Creates the dataset repo as private if it doesn't exist; if it already exists, it uploads into it.\n- Uploads `run_dir` to the root of the repo, skipping `*.tmp` files (at any depth) and everything under the top-level `wandb/` folder.\n- Takes an optional `api` argument, like `publish_summary` does.\n\n**Why it uses `upload_folder`:** in huggingface-hub 2.0.0, `upload_large_folder` has been removed completely, both the top-level function and the `HfApi` method. The installed library's docs say its large-folder handling moved into `HfApi.upload_folder`. With `hf_xet` present, `upload_folder` splits big folders into several commits to stay under the Hub's limits. If a job dies, calling it again skips files already on the Hub.\n\n**Decision for you:** without `hf_xet`, `upload_folder` falls back to a single commit. That can fail on tens of thousands of files and won't resume. `hf_xet` is normally installed automatically with huggingface-hub on x86_64 and arm64 machines, but it's missing from this `.venv`. So:\n- Check that the training machines have `hf_xet` installed and don't set `HF_HUB_DISABLE_XET`.\n- For now, `publish_run` just logs a warning if `hf_xet` is missing or disabled. If you'd rather it stop with an error in that case, that's a one-line change.\n\nI didn't install `hf_xet` or change any dependencies, as the project notes ask.\n\nI also added a test in `tests/test_publish.py`. It checks that the repo is created as private with `exist_ok`, that the upload goes to the repo root, and that the skip patterns drop `.tmp` and `wandb/` files but keep the others."
  },
